          <div class="section-label">Real-Life Examples</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Hospital System:</strong> A doctor and a nurse both log into the hospital system (both are authenticated). The doctor can view, edit, and prescribe medication records. The nurse can view records and add notes but cannot prescribe. Authorization defines these boundaries.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Office Building:</strong> An employee badge lets you enter the building (authentication). But only the CFO's badge authorizes access to the server room. Same building, different authorization levels.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Everyday App — Google Drive:</strong> The file owner can edit a shared document. A collaborator can comment. A viewer can only read. All three are authenticated users — their authorization level differs.</div>

          <div class="section-label">Step-by-Step Process</div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">User is first authenticated successfully</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">System checks user's roles or permissions</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">Request is compared against access policies</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">System allows or denies the specific action</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-text">Audit log records the decision</div></div>

          <div class="section-label">Strengths &amp; Weaknesses</div>
          <div class="pros-cons">
            <div class="pros">
              <div class="pros-title">Strengths</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Enforces data separation</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Limits blast radius of compromised accounts</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Enables fine-grained control</div>
            </div>
            <div class="cons">
              <div class="cons-title">Limitations</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Complex to manage at scale</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Misconfigured permissions cause breaches</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Hard to audit retrospectively</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="section-label">Related Terms</div>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">Permissions</span>
            <span class="tag">Policies</span>
            <span class="tag">Access Control</span>
            <span class="tag">Roles</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 3. AAA FRAMEWORK ─────────────────────── -->
      <section class="topic-section" id="aaa">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">
            <i class="ti ti-topology-star" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:20px"></i>
            AAA Framework
            <span class="badge badge-green">Framework</span>
          </div>
          <p class="definition">AAA stands for Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting — a three-pillar framework for controlling access to network resources. Together they answer: Who are you? What can you do? What did you do? It's the foundation of enterprise network security, especially in RADIUS and TACACS+ protocols.</p>

