          <div class="section-label">Real-Life Examples</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Online Banking:</strong> You enter your password (something you know), then a one-time code is sent to your registered phone (something you have). Both must be correct to access your account.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Accessing a Data Center:</strong> Employees swipe a key card (something you have) and scan their fingerprint (something you are) to enter a high-security server room. Two factors from two categories.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Everyday App — Microsoft 365:</strong> Enterprise users enter their password, then must approve a push notification on their Microsoft Authenticator app. This stops credential stuffing attacks even when passwords are leaked in data breaches.</div>

          <div class="section-label">Step-by-Step Process</div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">User enters primary credential (password)</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">System recognizes a second factor is required</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">Second factor prompt sent (SMS, app, biometric)</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">User provides the second factor</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-text">Both factors validated — access granted</div></div>

          <div class="section-label">Strengths &amp; Weaknesses</div>
          <div class="pros-cons">
            <div class="pros">
              <div class="pros-title">Strengths</div>
