              <text class="ts" x="192" y="80" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">FAR 0.1%</text>
              <text class="ts" x="192" y="95" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Contactless</text>
            </g>
            <g class="c-purple">
              <rect x="260" y="40" width="105" height="60" rx="8" stroke-width="0.5"></rect>
              <text class="th" x="312" y="62" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Iris</text>
              <text class="ts" x="312" y="80" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">FAR 0.0001%</text>
              <text class="ts" x="312" y="95" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Most accurate</text>
            </g>
            <g class="c-amber">
              <rect x="380" y="40" width="105" height="60" rx="8" stroke-width="0.5"></rect>
              <text class="th" x="432" y="62" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Voice</text>
              <text class="ts" x="432" y="80" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">FAR 1–5%</text>
              <text class="ts" x="432" y="95" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Phone banking</text>
            </g>
            <g class="c-coral">
              <rect x="500" y="40" width="120" height="60" rx="8" stroke-width="0.5"></rect>
              <text class="th" x="560" y="62" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Behavioral</text>
              <text class="ts" x="560" y="80" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Typing pattern</text>
              <text class="ts" x="560" y="95" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Continuous auth</text>
            </g>
            <text class="ts" x="320" y="136" text-anchor="middle">FAR = False Acceptance Rate (lower is more secure)</text>
            <text class="ts" x="320" y="154" text-anchor="middle">FRR = False Rejection Rate (lower is more convenient)</text>
            <text class="ts" x="320" y="172" text-anchor="middle">Security-convenience trade-off exists in all biometric systems</text>
          </svg>

          <div class="section-label">Real-Life Examples</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — iPhone Face ID:</strong> Your iPhone projects 30,000+ infrared dots onto your face, maps the 3D geometry, and compares it to the encrypted mathematical model stored in the Secure Enclave chip. No facial image is stored — only a mathematical representation.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Fingerprint in Airports:</strong> US Global Entry program uses fingerprint scans at immigration kiosks. Your fingerprint is matched against a pre-enrolled government database, allowing faster border crossing.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Banking Call Centers:</strong> Barclays Voice Banking analyzes 100+ voice characteristics (pitch, cadence, dialect) to authenticate callers in under 10 seconds — replacing PIN numbers for phone banking.</div>

          <div class="section-label">Step-by-Step Process</div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">Enrollment: biometric data captured and converted to a template</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">Template stored securely (often on device, not server)</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">At login, live biometric sample captured</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">System compares sample to stored template</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-text">Similarity score exceeds threshold — access granted</div></div>

          <div class="section-label">Strengths &amp; Weaknesses</div>
          <div class="pros-cons">
            <div class="pros">
              <div class="pros-title">Strengths</div>
