          <div class="section-label">Real-Life Examples</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — AWS IAM:</strong> Instead of attaching S3 read/write permissions directly to individual developers, you create a "DeveloperRole" containing S3 policies and assign developers to it.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — WordPress:</strong> WordPress manages access via roles like Administrator (full access), Editor (can publish and edit anyone's posts), Author (can write and publish own posts), and Subscriber (read-only).</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Everyday App — GitHub:</strong> Repositories have roles like Owner, Collaborator, and Viewer. You add users to a team which is assigned a specific repository access role.</div>

          <div class="section-label">Step-by-Step Process</div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">Identify system permissions (actions like Read, Write, Delete)</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">Define roles based on job functions (Admin, Editor, Support)</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">Assign the permissions to those roles</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">Map users to one or more appropriate roles</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-text">Auth server checks roles to allow or deny actions</div></div>

