          <div class="section-label">Real-Life Examples</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Sign in with Google:</strong> When you open a site like Medium or Airbnb, you click "Sign in with Google." Google verifies your credentials, asks for your consent, and sends a token confirming your identity to Medium/Airbnb.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Corporate Okta Portal:</strong> An employee logs into their Okta dashboard once in the morning. They can click on icons for Salesforce, Gmail, Workday, and Jira, launching them directly without entering passwords again.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Everyday App — Microsoft Entra ID:</strong> Enterprise employees sign in once to Windows, gaining seamless access to Outlook, Teams, SharePoint, and internal business apps automatically.</div>

          <div class="section-label">Step-by-Step Process</div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">User accesses the Service Provider app (e.g. Slack) and clicks Login</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">Slack redirects the browser to the Identity Provider (e.g. Okta) with an auth request</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">User authenticates directly with Okta (entering password + MFA)</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">Okta generates a signed token (assertion) and redirects back to Slack</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-text">Slack validates the signature on the token and logs the user in</div></div>

          <div class="section-label">Strengths &amp; Weaknesses</div>
          <div class="pros-cons">
            <div class="pros">
              <div class="pros-title">Strengths</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Increases user productivity and convenience</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Centralizes credentials (no password reuse across apps)</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Allows central enforcement of MFA policies</div>
            </div>
            <div class="cons">
              <div class="cons-title">Limitations</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Single point of failure (if IdP goes down, all apps block)</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− "Single point of compromise" (stolen IdP account gives access to all apps)</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Complex initial protocol configuration (SAML metadata/certs)</div>
            </div>
          </div>

