            </g>
            <text class="ts" x="490" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--green)">If compromised: Production DB is safe!</text>
          </svg>

          <div class="section-label">Real-Life Examples</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Local Admin Rights:</strong> A standard employee laptop does not have local admin privileges. Employees can run apps but cannot install software or modify registry settings without IT approval. This stops drive-by malware.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — Docker Containers:</strong> Best practices dictate running Docker containers as a non-root user (e.g., `USER node` in Node.js apps). If a container is exploited, the hacker gets no host root privileges.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Everyday App — Mobile App Permissions:</strong> An email client asks for permission to access contacts but doesn't ask for microphone, camera, or location settings. It requesting only what is needed.</div>

          <div class="section-label">Step-by-Step Process</div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">Map out the specific tasks that a user or service account must do</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">Identify the bare minimum privileges required for those tasks</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">Configure permissions default-deny (start with zero access)</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">Add privileges incrementally until task needs are exactly met</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-text">Audit and review permissions regularly, stripping unused ones</div></div>

          <div class="section-label">Strengths &amp; Weaknesses</div>
          <div class="pros-cons">
            <div class="pros">
              <div class="pros-title">Strengths</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Restricts blast radius of any compromised asset</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Mitigates insider threats and accident risks</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Greatly simplifies compliance audits</div>
            </div>
            <div class="cons">
              <div class="cons-title">Limitations</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Increases support tickets (users asking for access)</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Requires constant tracking of changing job duties</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Can temporarily slow down development speed</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="section-label">Related Terms</div>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">Default Deny</span>
            <span class="tag">Blast Radius</span>
            <span class="tag">Segregation of Duties</span>
            <span class="tag">Need to Know</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 12. ACCESS TOKENS (JWT & SESSIONS) ───── -->
      <section class="topic-section" id="accesstokens">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">
            <i class="ti ti-id-badge" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:20px"></i>
            Access Tokens (JWT &amp; Sessions)
            <span class="badge badge-green">Mechanism</span>
          </div>
          <p class="definition">Access Tokens are keys used by applications to prove that a client has been authenticated and is authorized to execute API requests. They are typically stateless self-contained JSON Web Tokens (JWT) signed by a server key, or reference tokens referring to data stored in a database session table.</p>

          <div class="section-label">How it works — JWT Structure</div>
          <svg class="flow-svg" viewBox="0 0 640 180" role="img">
            <title>JWT Structure</title>
            <defs>
              <marker id="arr12" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
                <path d="M2 1L8 5L2 9" fill="none" stroke="#8b96a5" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
              </marker>
            </defs>
            <text class="th" x="320" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Anatomy of a JWT (JSON Web Token)</text>
            
            <g class="c-red">
              <rect x="20" y="45" width="180" height="80" rx="8" stroke-width="0.5"></rect>
              <text class="th" x="110" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Header (Red)</text>
              <text class="ts" x="110" y="85" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Algorithm &amp; Token Type</text>
              <text class="ts" x="110" y="105" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">{"alg": "HS256", "typ": "JWT"}</text>
            </g>
            <text class="th" x="210" y="85" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text3)">.</text>

            <g class="c-purple">
              <rect x="220" y="45" width="200" height="80" rx="8" stroke-width="0.5"></rect>
              <text class="th" x="320" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Payload (Purple)</text>
              <text class="ts" x="320" y="85" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Claims (subject, role, exp)</text>
              <text class="ts" x="320" y="105" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">{"sub": "123", "role": "editor"}</text>
            </g>
            <text class="th" x="430" y="85" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text3)">.</text>

            <g class="c-blue">
              <rect x="440" y="45" width="180" height="80" rx="8" stroke-width="0.5"></rect>
              <text class="th" x="530" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Signature (Blue)</text>
              <text class="ts" x="530" y="85" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">HMAC-SHA256 Hash</text>
              <text class="ts" x="530" y="105" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Prevents tampering</text>
            </g>
            <text class="ts" x="320" y="150" text-anchor="middle">Base64UrlEncodedHeader . Base64UrlEncodedPayload . CryptographicSignature</text>
          </svg>

          <div class="section-label">Real-Life Examples</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — REST APIs:</strong> A user logs in. The server returns a JWT. For all subsequent requests, the user's browser adds the header: `Authorization: Bearer <jwt_token>`. The server decodes it and grants access without querying a DB.</jwt_token></div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Real Life — SSO Tokens:</strong> SSO architectures pass identity profiles in tokens between the Identity Provider (IdP) and Service Providers. OIDC uses ID tokens (JWTs) to carry verified user profiles.</div>
          <div class="example-box"><strong>Everyday App — Auth Cookies:</strong> When you login to a shopping site, a session cookie (containing a random session ID) is set in your browser. The server maps this ID to your session record in Redis to authenticate your shopping cart.</div>

          <div class="section-label">Step-by-Step Process</div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">User logs in with credentials. Authentication succeeds</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">Server generates a token containing user data and signs it with a secret key</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">Server responds back, sending the token to the client</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">Client stores it (localStorage or HTTP-only cookies) and sends it in API headers</div></div>
          <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-text">API gateway verifies the token's cryptographic signature and validates expiration</div></div>

          <div class="section-label">Strengths &amp; Weaknesses</div>
          <div class="pros-cons">
            <div class="pros">
              <div class="pros-title">Strengths</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Stateless validation (servers don't need a DB lookup)</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Standardized structure (JSON, easy to pass in headers)</div>
              <div class="pc-item">+ Works across different domains and microservices</div>
            </div>
            <div class="cons">
              <div class="cons-title">Limitations</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Revocation is difficult before token naturally expires</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− If key is compromised, attackers can mint arbitrary tokens</div>
              <div class="pc-item">− Storing in localStorage exposes token to XSS attacks</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="section-label">Related Terms</div>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">JWT</span>
            <span class="tag">Bearer Token</span>
            <span class="tag">Access Token</span>
            <span class="tag">Refresh Token</span>
            <span class="tag">HMAC-SHA256</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

